PEAK HEAP AFTER THE TWO ALLOCATION-SITE HOISTS — AND IT IS A NULL, AS THE
LIFETIME ARGUMENT PREDICTED.

date:   2026-09-03
host:   r7900x — AMD Ryzen 7900X, 24 threads, x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu, 29 GiB.
        NOT the aarch64 box the RSS records use. This is HEAP, not RSS.
        The box was concurrently running this commit's own x86 gate suite;
        that affects wall clock, which this file does not report, and not heap
        bytes, which are load-independent (same caveat as
        benchmarks/mem_heaptrack_arena_2026-09-03.meta).
tool:   heaptrack + heaptrack_print ("peak heap memory consumption").
cell:   gradient <size>x<size> qp 40 preset 13, 8-bit 4:2:0, ONE encode per
        cell (not a median). Sizes 1280 / 1536 / 1920 / 2048.
arms:   still    = perf_encode default (avif still config), 1 frame
        videokey = SVTAV1_VIDEO=1, 1 frame
        inter    = SVTAV1_VIDEO=1 SVTAV1_FRAMES=2 SVTAV1_FRAME_SHIFT=3
        REFUSAL TRAP (mem_heaptrack_2026-09-03.meta): the harness checks each
        cell's `.obu` is non-empty BEFORE reading its peak, and prints
        NO-OBU-REFUSED instead of a number if it is not. Every one of the 12
        cells below produced an `.obu` (70,893-139,762 bytes).
trees:  BEFORE = 061aae79 (the tile-buffer reservation) — the RESRV column of
                 benchmarks/mem_heaptrack_arena_2026-09-03.meta
        AFTER  = fbd341b37 = BEFORE + 9b323c6f (docs)
                 + 58fa779e (the RDOQ trellis's `dqcoeff` hoisted into
                   `TxScratch`)
                 + fbd341b3 (`txb_coeff_satd`'s two buffers hoisted into a
                   per-thread scratch)

======================= PEAK HEAP, MiB =======================

  size  arm        BEFORE    AFTER     AFTER/BEFORE
  1280  still       32.26     32.26    1.000
  1280  videokey    38.66     38.67    1.000
  1280  inter       55.89     55.90    1.000
  1536  still       45.95     45.95    1.000
  1536  videokey    54.71     54.72    1.000
  1536  inter       79.57     79.58    1.000
  1920  still       71.17     71.18    1.000
  1920  videokey    84.10     84.11    1.000
  1920  inter      123.01    123.02    1.000
  2048  still       80.79     80.80    1.000
  2048  videokey    95.68     95.68    1.000
  2048  inter      139.61    139.62    1.000

Ten of the twelve cells read +0.01 M and two read unchanged; +0.01 M at
heaptrack_print's two-decimal MiB resolution is the smallest non-zero
difference the tool can print. **The change is NULL to within 0.01 %.**

======================= WHAT THIS SAYS =======================

1. **TWO MEASURED CPU WINS BOUGHT ZERO PEAK HEAP, and that is the expected
   result, not a surprise.** Both commits remove allocator CHURN — 72,919
   calls at `tx_pipeline::tx_unit_inner` and 28,006 at
   `leaf_funnel::detect::txb_coeff_satd` on one 512x512 video-mode key frame
   at preset 8 — from buffers whose LIFETIME is one transform unit. A buffer
   that is freed before the next one is allocated was never part of the peak.
   This is the same finding `mem_heaptrack_arena_2026-09-03.meta` records for
   the PA/ME pooling, reached from the opposite direction: there the pooling
   was a CPU null AND a memory null; here the hoists are a CPU win and still a
   memory null.

2. **THE INTER FRAME'S MARGINAL HEAP COST HAS NOT MOVED.** At 2048x2048, by
   subtraction of the arms (a subtraction of measured quantities):

     what the VIDEO config adds:   port +14.88 M   (was +14.89 M)
     what ONE INTER FRAME adds:    port +43.94 M   (was +43.93 M)
     minus the harness's own extra input frame (`perf_encode::translate`,
     6.29 MB exactly, measured in mem_heaptrack_2026-09-03.meta):

       ONE INTER FRAME, ENCODER-SIDE:  port +37.65 M   against C's +0.01 M

   The C side is unchanged by construction — no C code changed — so the
   port/C inter-frame heap ratio stands where mem_heaptrack_2026-09-03.meta
   left it.

3. **The memory axis therefore still needs the LIFETIME change, not more
   allocation-shape changes.** The port holds the whole frame's decision tree,
   with a coefficient `Vec` per block, until the entropy walk; C packs a
   superblock and releases its buffers. `funnel_block_decision` (16.79 M over
   4096 calls) and the `Vec<PartitionTree>` collect in `encode_tile_rows`
   (11.53 M over 4110) are that one structure seen from two sites. Hoisting
   more non-escaping scratch will keep paying in CPU and keep paying nothing
   in peak heap.

======================= NOT ESTABLISHED =======================

  * Any RSS number. This is heap.
  * Any aarch64 number. Different allocator, different page size.
  * Repeat variance: one encode per cell, as in the record it compares to.
  * Any preset but 13, content but gradient, qp but 40.
  * That the CPU wins are null too — they are not. See
    benchmarks/txscratch_dqcoeff_ab_2026-09-03.* and
    benchmarks/satdscratch_ab_2026-09-03.*, both paired A/Bs on aarch64.
